ne or alcohol all you needed was a  government certificate which was easy to  obtain if you bribed the person that was  responsible for making them the way it  worked it was relatively simple remus or  one of his associates would buy a  shuttered distillery often for pennies  on the dollar as the despondent owners  were happy to collect at least some  money on what was now apparently useless  property sometimes remus would restart  production under the guise of producing  medical grade bourbon and sometimes he  just bought the property to empty out  the storehouse of existing inventory  using his knowledge of the pharmacy  industry remus would open up drug stores  that served as a front for his bourbon  shipments most of which never made it to  the stated destination instead it was  reported missing lost or stolen when in  reality it was actually being taken to a  secret distribution point just outside  of cincinnati from there it was sold to  other bootleggers who transported it out  of town to sell it to individual  customers remus was successful for two  reasons quantity and quality his network  had access to millions of gallons of  whiskey far more than anyone else  allowing him to corner the market and  the bourbon was the good stuff because  unlike many other bootleggers remus  refused to cut his product in order to  make a bigger profit if you were lucky  all they did was water it down but if  you are unlucky the liquor you bought  might be mixed with industrial alcohol  or sterno used as a heating fuel or  something else that would be more likely  to poison you than get you drunk  prohibition newspapers were filled with  stories of people who died after  drinking tainted alcohol no such issue  with remus product in a matter of months  remus was the head of a huge enterprise  employing hundreds of workers and  serving as the primary supplier of  alcohol to the country's two largest  cities new york and chicago he  established relationships with local  gangster kingpins including arnold  rothstein in new york and johnny torrio  in chicago after torrio was shot in 1925  he retired and handed the reigns over to  his lieutenant al capone the jazz ages  most notorious criminal unlike capone  who ruled through fear ramus didn't need  to resort to violence in the name of  business ramus believed that every man  had his price and he had so much money  that he was perfectly willing to pay  that price he bribed everyone local cops  prohibition agents politicians civil  servants night watchmen farmers anyone  that he needed to his web of graft  extended all the way to washington with  tens of thousands of dollars paid to  jess smith the assistant and bag man for  attorney general harry dougherty that  didn't mean his enterprise was a  peaceful one however bootlegging was a  dangerous business after all each one of  remus's shipments was accompanied by a  car load of well-armed gunmen to fend  off would-be hijackers and his own  private army protected the primary  distribution point which quickly became  known as death valley because of what  would happen to anyone that showed up  and attempted to steal remus liquor  [Music]  george dreamers had succeeded beyond his  wildest dreams and he and his new wife  imogen were quick to take advantage of  their newfound fortune the couple lived  in a huge mansion in cincinnati's  fashionable price hill neighborhood and  dreamers did business in his own  six-story office building downtown just  like the captains of industry he modeled  himself after his home life was like a  real-life version of the great gatsby as  george and imogen filled their home with  whatever opulent thing happened to catch  their eye furniture artwork marble  statues sculptures jewelry tailored  clothing furs carpets so much stuff that  it didn't all fit in and had to be  stored in the attic they owned a fleet  of cars to take them wherever they  wanted to go and spent at least 150 000  on a luxurious indoor pool and 