r kingpins including arnold  rothstein in new york and johnny torrio  in chicago after torrio was shot in 1925  he retired and handed the reigns over to  his lieutenant al capone the jazz ages  most notorious criminal unlike capone  who ruled through fear ramus didn't need  to resort to violence in the name of  business ramus believed that every man  had his price and he had so much money  that he was perfectly willing to pay  that price he bribed everyone local cops  prohibition agents politicians civil  servants night watchmen farmers anyone  that he needed to his web of graft  extended all the way to washington with  tens of thousands of dollars paid to  jess smith the assistant and bag man for  attorney general harry dougherty that  didn't mean his enterprise was a  peaceful one however bootlegging was a  dangerous business after all each one of  remus's shipments was accompanied by a  car load of well-armed gunmen to fend  off would-be hijackers and his own  private army protected the primary  distribution point which quickly became  known as death valley because of what  would happen to anyone that showed up  and attempted to steal remus liquor  [Music]  george dreamers had succeeded beyond his  wildest dreams and he and his new wife  imogen were quick to take advantage of  their newfound fortune the couple lived  in a huge mansion in cincinnati's  fashionable price hill neighborhood and  dreamers did business in his own  six-story office building downtown just  like the captains of industry he modeled  himself after his home life was like a  real-life version of the great gatsby as  george and imogen filled their home with  whatever opulent thing happened to catch  their eye furniture artwork marble  statues sculptures jewelry tailored  clothing furs carpets so much stuff that  it didn't all fit in and had to be  stored in the attic they owned a fleet  of cars to take them wherever they  wanted to go and spent at least 150 000  on a luxurious indoor pool and bath  house complex in the mansion and still  he found himself with more money he  literally had more cash than things to  spend it on to go with his newfound  riches he formed an eccentric  personality that made him stand out in  both the criminal underworld and the  privileged society to which he attempted  to purchase membership he always carried  an ornate walking stick with a heavy  head good for both showcasing his  sophistication and for clubbing people  over the head when he lost his temper  with them since his business ran  primarily on cash transactions and remus  distrusted banks he was known to carry  large amounts of currency on his person  at all times to the point of it  sometimes falling out of his coat  pockets one of his most unique traits  was a tendency to refer to himself in  the third person a personality quirk  known as ilism he likes to call himself  remus especially when talking to  newspaper reporters who he spent a lot  of time with despite the clandestine  nature of his business not even remus  himself knew how much money he made or  spent since he didn't keep account books  or write anything down that would be  incriminating evidence if authorities  ever got a hold of it but it is believed  that his bourbon empire sold whiskey  worth hundreds of millions of dollars  several billion dollars in today's money  and remus got the biggest piece of that  pie since he ran the entire organization  remus was certainly one of the richest  criminals in america and one of the most  successful bootleggers perhaps in  hindsight he was a bit too successful in  washington dc the head of the  prohibition bureau mabel walker  willowbrand was fuming it seemed like  nobody was taking prohibition seriously  at least of all those charged with  enforcing the law the bureau was  understaffed underfunded overwhelmed and  riddled with corruption the scale of the  law breaking had clearly taken everyone  by surprise and the government was slow  to respond as deputy attorney general  willa brandt was the most powerful woman  in america and she still had greater  ambitions like becoming the first woman  appointed to the supreme court  prohibition enforcement had to be  improved if her political goals would be  realized what she needed was a  high-profile success something to splash  around the nation's newspapers and give  her the positive publicity that she  needed to gain credibility she had to  take down a kingpin  [Music]  in october 1921 death valley was raided  by prohibition agents despite remus  instructions his underlings left a lot  of incriminating evidence relying around  which was seized remus was arrested and  charged with violating the bolstered act  released on bond the bootlegger went  about his business with little concern  for the upcoming trial he figured that  his bribery network was extensive enough  that he would be protected from prison  he was so unworried that on new year's  eve he hosted a lavish party for 300  guests in which diamond stickmans and  cars were given away as party favors but  mabel willibrandt had her sights set on  remus she was determined to string him  up as a poster child for a cause to  showcase to america that prohibition was  working in may 1922 remus was convicted  and sentenced to two years in federal  prison at another trial later the month  he was sentenced to an additional year  in prison in ohio remus remained free  while he appealed his conviction certain  that his friends in high places would  intercede on his behalf what remus  didn't realize however was that the  administration of president warren g  harding was about to completely fall  apart harding himself is not known to  have been corrupt but the people around  him collectively known as the ohio gang  made graft an art form enriching  themselves by misusing the power of  their officers accepting bribes and  orchestrating insider deals at  government expense in 1923 a thread was  pulled and the scheme began to unravel a  senate investigation into the department  of justice was turning up unpleasant  facts about the attorney general and his  aide jess smith harding told secretary  doughty to cut his friend smith loose to  save himself despondent smith committed  suicide on may 30 1923 two months later  on august the second president harding  himself died plunging the nation into  mourning harry dougherty was ousted as  attorney general by president calvin  coolidge in 1924 at the same time remus  had run out of appeals as they had all  been rejected all of his bribes all the  assurances he'd received from public  officials all of it had come to nothing  he was incarcerated at the atlanta  federal penitentiary in january 1924  while remus was in prison his wife  imogen was left in charge of all of his  assets this suited her just fine as she  loved the opulent lifestyle that she'd  become accustomed to since 1920. she'd  dumped her first husband for remus  because she believed he could provide  her with a better quality of life and  now here she was in charge of billions  of dollars of untraceable money and  valuables it was almost too good to  resist especially once she met and fell  in love with a man named franklin dodge  jr dodge ironically enough was a  prohibition agents and not just any  prohibition agent either he had played  an instrumental part in putting george  remus in prison now is not only going to  steal remus wife but all of his money  too imagine the bootlegger surprise when  he was released from prison in september  1925 only to be hit with divorce papers  from his supposed loving wife his shock  got even worse when he arrived back in  cincinnati to discover his mansion  stripped down to the floorboards as  imogen and dodge had carried off all of  his valuables they'd spent the last two  years systematically plundering what was  left of his empire living the high life  while george rotted in prison and the  worst part was little george could do  about it especially when he ended up  back in jail in ohio from july 1926 to  april 1927 for the second conviction  after those appeals were also exhausted  all the while acrimonious divorce  proceedings dragged their way through  the court system as imogen attempted to  take george for all that he had and  george striking back alleging imogen had  participated in illegal activities as  part of his bootlegging business as well  as accusing her of adultery which was  still a prosecutable offense in those  days especially for a woman things got  increasingly ugly rumors swirled that  dodge and imogen were offering a 15 000  bounty to anyone that would kill remus  george's friends reported that he was  becoming increasingly unhinged following  dodge and imaging around and ranting  about getting revenge despite his  outward image as a gentleman remus was  known for losing his temper and lashing  out both of his wives had alleged it  struck them on multiple occasions and he  had been arrested several times for  assault things reached a breaking point  on october 6 1927. that morning george  watched from a car as his wife and  step-daughter ruth climbed into a  taxicab and headed downtown for a  meeting with imogen's attorney as the  cab drove off remus's car followed remus  chased the cab down the street running  it off the road and into the grass of  the lush eden park remus car forced the  cab to stop next to a lake and a gazebo  a popular picnic spot and play area for  cincinnati families imogen fled from the  car chased by george who caught up to  her and grabbed her remus cursed his  wife punched her in the face before  pulling a pearl handled pistol from his  coat pocket in front of dozens of  shocked onlookers remus fired a single  shot into imogen's abdomen and then fled  the scene on foot two hours later the  imaginary must died on the operating  table at the same time jaw dreamers had  turned himself in to cincinnati police  detectives and confessed to the shooting  the bourbon king  is now on the hook for murder  [Music]  remus was now in for the fight of his  life literally conviction almost  certainly meant death in ohio's electric  chair accordingly he pulled out all the  stops to try and save himself no one  could deny that he had shot and killed  his wife the evidence was overwhelming  reimus defense therefore borrowed from a  tactic he'd used over a decade earlier  when defending a man accused of wife  slaying in chicago a temporary insanity  defense his contention was that his  wife's actions had driven him literally  crazy to the point of shooting her  something he wouldn't have done if he'd  been in his right mind and therefore he  shouldn't be held responsible for his  actions that day remus acted as his own  defense attorney throughout the trial  facing off against hamilton county chief  prosecutor charles p taft ii charles was  the youngest son of william howard taft  the only man to have held both the  office of the president and of united  states chief justice of the supreme  court the sensational trial played out  in the courtroom and in the newspapers  remus utilizing his connections with  reporters to sway public opinion to his  side he put the victim on trial smearing  imogen and franklin dodge as players in  a nefarious plot to destroy him he  showed conclusive evidence that the two  of them were having an affair and that  they'd conspired to steal his money and  property he made other accusations as  well they were trying to get him locked  up deported back to germany or even  trying to kill him taft countered by  arguing that it didn't matter what  imogen did to george that didn't give  him the right to kill her he brought her  parade of alienists the 1920s tied what  we today call a psychiatrist to testify  that remus had always been sane  including on the day of the murder but  he was always fighting an uphill battle  prohibition was growing increasingly  unpopular and those who provided the  masses with bootleg liquor was seen as a  kind of folk hero the court of public  opinion was firmly on remus's side from  the start which surely had an effect on  the jury on december 20th 1927 they  found remus not guilty by reason of  temporary insanity they had deliberated  for only 19 minutes  rhema spent six months at the state  hospital for the criminally insane while  lawyers battled in the courtroom about  whether he was actually insane he was  released after the ohio supreme court  determined that he wasn't and just like  that remus was a free man well he may  have been free but the bourbon empire  that had built was gone he wasn't  completely broke but the millions had  earned had disappeared seized by  government officials or squandered by  his wife and a lover and he had no taste  to try and restart it either the bootleg  gangs had risen to power while he was in  prison and had grown increasingly  violent and were drawing more and more  negative attention to themselves he was  also too well known not only in the  media but also to prohibition agents to  escape attention for long if he tried to  do anything else illegal prohibition  ended on december 5th 1933 with the  ratification of the 21st amendment the  only constitutional amendment to repeal  an earlier one individual states  continued to ban alcohol for decades  after national prohibition ended the  last tri-state mississippi went wet in  1966. the organized crime rackets that  had sprung up around bootlegging didn't  disappear after prohibition ended they  simply transitioned into other markets  like prostitution gambling becoming  what's today simply known as the mob  george rumors lived the rest of his life  in relative obscurity dying in 1952 at  the age of 75. his story is largely  unknown eclipsed by the more well-known  story of al capone and his battle with  elliot ness and his untouchables in many  ways the jaw dreamer story perfectly  encapsulates the roaring twenties a  sudden rise to unparalleled prosperity  conspicuous consumption brutal violence  underneath a veneer of sophistication  and a sudden fall from grace losing it  all and having to start over again draw  dreamus was many things throughout his  life pharmacist lawyer convict the man  who got away with murder but for one  brief shining moment draw dreamus was  the king of the bootleggers the evil  genius of prohibition the last  alastrimus had to find out the hard way  what all of us learn sooner or later  all good things must eventually  come to an end  [Music]
 

